    "authorNotes": "The letter-shape explanations follow the Haerye's 'Jejahae' (제자해) as summarized by 한국민족문화대백과사전. The Haerye's yin-yang and five-elements philosophy is left out as too advanced. The add-a-stroke rule has exceptions (ㆁ, ㄹ, ㅿ, per 한국민족문화대백과사전), so the narration gives only the ㄴ→ㄷ→ㅌ example. The narration says 'a few old ones are no longer used' rather than naming exactly four, because ㆁ merged with ㅇ rather than disappearing. The 국립국어원 essay (004d/004g) notes that a few details of the design principle are still debated among scholars; the story keeps to the consensus points. Because illustrations must contain no text, the app should overlay the jamo (ㄱ, ㄴ, ㅏ and so on) as UI text on the scene art. REVISED: the shape descriptions now follow the Haerye wording exactly (ㄱ = root of the tongue closing the throat, 象舌根閉喉之形; ㄴ = tongue touching the upper gum, 象舌附上腭之形) and no longer promise a physical sensation or add modern phonetics ('back of the tongue blocks the throat', 'ridge behind the upper teeth' removed; narration and illustration). The summary now says 'five basic consonants' (only ㄱ ㄴ ㅁ ㅅ ㅇ copy speech organs; others add strokes). Scene 2 no longer lists 'lips' (the Haerye names tongue root, tongue, mouth, tooth, throat). Vocab 혀 removed because no scene shows it. FINAL PASS (3rd check): scene 8, goal 4 and f7 now say South Korea's spelling rules count 24 (North Korea's norms count 40). The ㄴ wording follows the standard Korean rendering of 象舌附上腭之形 ('tongue touching the upper gum'); the Chinese 上腭 literally means the upper palate/jaw, so no finer anatomical claim is made.",
